:name: daisy-boot with app in QSPI
:description: Boot the daisy-rs bootloader in Renode with daisy-app-template
:description: preloaded in QSPI, and trace execution through the alive-blink
:description: window and the jump to the app. Used to diagnose "bootloader
:description: jumps but app doesn't run" faults without further hardware flash
:description: cycles.
:origin: daisy-rs/renode

$name?="daisy-boot-jump"

mach create $name
machine LoadPlatformDescription $ORIGIN/daisy_seed.repl

# Bootloader in internal flash.
$boot=$ORIGIN/../target/thumbv7em-none-eabihf/release/daisy-boot
# App template — its vector table sits at 0x9000_0000 (matches memory.x
# for the app). Renode's LoadELF places sections at their VMA, so this
# preloads QSPI with the same bytes our DFU service would write.
$app=$ORIGIN/../target/thumbv7em-none-eabihf/release/daisy-app-template

sysbus LoadELF $boot
sysbus LoadELF $app

# Renode's VectorTableOffset auto-guess picks the first RAM block loaded
# from an ELF (bootloader's zero-init EP_MEMORY at 0x24000000). That
# reads back zeros for MSP/PC and halts the CPU. Force VTOR to the
# bootloader's real vector table and manually set the initial PC + SP.
cpu VectorTableOffset 0x08000000
sysbus.cpu PC `sysbus GetSymbolAddress "Reset"`
sysbus.cpu SP `sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x08000000`

# NOTE: enabling `cpu LogFunctionNames true` slows emulation to a crawl
# because every function entry is logged. Enable it for a specific short
# window when you actually need the trace. Left disabled by default so
# `RunFor` completes in a reasonable wall-clock time.

# Do NOT start emulation here — the Robot test drives that.
